{
  // Holding_Guard
  // Value: block reward
  // The script a holding box actually carries. It holds the hash of the real holding logic,
  // dispatches on an op byte, and runs that logic — supplied at spend time and checked against the
  // hash before a single line of it is trusted.
  //
  // Size is the whole point, and it is paid in an unusual place. This script is serialized into the
  // genesis transaction as an output, the genesis transaction is carried whole inside every
  // super-share's TransactionProof, and a NISP holds ten super-shares — so one byte here is ten
  // bytes of NISP, and a fraud proof carries three NISPs. Every byte moved out of this script and
  // into a context variable is thirty bytes off the fraud-proof worst case, and it is paid instead
  // by the submission transaction that actually uses it.
  //
  // Same trade as LD_Provision_Guard and Emission_Guard, with one structural difference forced on
  // it: executeFromVar CANNOT NEST. The holding logic used to run the miner's own signing script
  // out of var 0, and it cannot do that from inside a script that is itself being executed from a
  // variable. So the miner's script is run HERE, and the tree key is bound HERE, and the logic
  // never sees the signer at all — it takes CTX_NISP_DATA._1 as already authenticated.
  //
  // That is also why the op byte exists. The phase used to be chosen by an if on HEIGHT, which the
  // logic could evaluate for itself; but this script has to know, before it executes anything,
  // whether a signer script is going to be run. So the spender declares the path and BOTH scripts
  // branch on the same variable. The declaration buys nothing on its own: the logic re-checks the
  // height condition that the old if provided, as an ordinary conjunct on the submission path.

  // CONSTANTS
  // CONST_HOLDING_LOGIC_HASH: blake2b256 of the serialized value bytes of the holding logic

  // CONTEXT VARS
  // CTX_SIGNER(0): value bytes of the miner's signing proposition. Submission only.
  // CTX_NISP_DATA(1): ( hashed miner proposition bytes -> NISP bytes ). Submission only.
  // CTX_OP(3): 0 submit, 1 transform. Read by this script and by the logic, which see the same value.
  // CTX_LOGIC(64): serialized value bytes of the holding logic.

  // ASSUMPTIONS - statements which can be assumed to be true due to other contracts enforcing them
  // during creation of this UTXO
  // Collateral_Mainnet pins blake2b256 of this script as the holding box's proposition, so a box
  // under this script is the rollup the collateral transaction opened.
  //
  // DELIBERATE OMISSIONS - conditions left out on purpose
  // Nothing here looks at registers, tokens, values or outputs. Every rule about what a holding box
  // is and what may be done to it belongs to the logic; duplicating any of it here would create a
  // second place to keep in step, and cost the bytes this contract exists to save.

  val CTX_OP    = getVar[Byte](3).getOrElse(-1.toByte)
  val CTX_LOGIC = getVar[Coll[Byte]](64).get

  val authenticLogic = blake2b256( CTX_LOGIC ) == CONST_HOLDING_LOGIC_HASH

  // Spending Path 1: a miner submits a NISP, and signs for it
  if(CTX_OP == 0.toByte){
    val CTX_NISP_DATA = getVar[(Coll[Byte], Coll[Byte])](1).getOrElse((Coll(0.toByte), Coll(0.toByte)))
    val signerProp = executeFromVar[SigmaProp](0)
    // The tree key is the hash of the miner's proposition, so binding it here is what stops one
    // miner submitting under another's identity. It lives in this script only because the logic
    // cannot execute var 0 for itself.
    val authenticMiner = blake2b256( signerProp.propBytes ) == CTX_NISP_DATA._1

    sigmaProp(authenticLogic && authenticMiner) && signerProp && executeFromVar[SigmaProp](64)
  }else if(CTX_OP == 1.toByte){
    // Spending Path 2: transform into the evaluation contract. No signer, no identity to bind.
    sigmaProp(authenticLogic) && executeFromVar[SigmaProp](64)
  }else{
    sigmaProp(false)
  }
}
